I checked both passages against the page image and the full-text dump named in each header. I compared them word by word, by eye and with a script-based diff of each paragraph against the dump.

z98 (2026 Shandong reading C): the original has 5 paragraphs. All 5 were compared, and the ¶ count and breaks match. The passage starts at 'Ms. Beryl and Layla…', directly after the 'C篇' heading, and ends at '…since we will all be old one day.”', directly before question 8. No question text is mixed in, and the Chinese glosses (有益的) and (共情) are correct. The diff found exactly 4 transcription differences, all reported: 78 vs printed 87; 'interest' vs 'interests'; 'each others’' vs 'each other’s'; and 'listen them' vs 'listen to them'. These look like planted errors, and all four are definite. In the paper itself I also flagged the comma in 'says director, Penny Livingston' (low). I kept the following. 'Ms. Beryl', a preschool form of address and part of the original. 'The pair have', British collective usage. 'programmes', British spelling. 'The programmes work and even the kids’ parents benefit', two short coordinated clauses where the comma is optional. 'that Beryl is 87, and Layla is four', which is acceptable.

z35 (2025 Beijing reading D): the original has 5 paragraphs. All 5 were compared, and the ¶ count and breaks match. The current text is identical to the page and the dump, character for character, including (AI), the glosses (数据)(算法)(偏见)(反映)(原则), the hyphens in 'AI-powered', 'long-standing' and 'money-lending', and the apostrophes. The passage starts at 'People are talking…' and ends at '…for what we see.', which is the whole one-page paper with no question text. So there is no transcription error. I reported four errors in the paper itself:
- the comma splice 'this way, instead, it was' (medium);
- 'colored people' → 'people of color' (medium; the term is offensive);
- 'in money-lending business' → 'in the money-lending business' (low);
- the comma splice 'new biases (偏见), it is mirroring' (low).

I looked closely at these and kept them as legitimate adapted or simplified wording:
- 'By making these problems seen and more pressing' (awkward, but understandable);
- 'They are judging the data, rules, and beliefs';
- 'the footprints of ourselves we leave in the world';
- 'take increasing care to read our principles (原则) into the record of our actions rather than allowing the two to diverge' (the source's metaphor; 'diverge' is the word underlined in the paper);
- 'how we as average people want to shape its role';
- 'It isn’t enough to ask how it will change us' ('it' clearly refers to AI).

A web search did not find the exact source essay, so the paper-level findings rest on standard grammar and usage. No files were modified.
